.TH SCALBN 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
scalbn, scalbnf, scalbnl, scalbln, scalblnf, scalblnl \- scale floating-point number by integer power of radix
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double scalbn(double " x ", int " n );
.br
.BI "float scalbnf(float " x ", int " n );
.br
.BI "long double scalbnl(long double " x ", int " n );
.PP
.BI "double scalbln(double " x ", long " n );
.br
.BI "float scalblnf(float " x ", long " n );
.br
.BI "long double scalblnl(long double " x ", long " n );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR scalbn ()
function computes the value of
.I x
multiplied by
.B FLT_RADIX
raised to the power
.IR n :
.PP
.RS 4
.IR x " * FLT_RADIX^" n .
.RE
.PP
On Substrate and other implementations where
.B FLT_RADIX
is 2 (as required by IEC 60559 / IEEE\ 754 binary floating point), the
result is identical to that of
.PP
.RS 4
.BI "ldexp(" x ", " n ) .
.RE
.PP
The
.BR scalbln ()
function is identical to
.BR scalbn (),
except that the exponent argument
.I n
has type
.B long
instead of
.BR int .
This variant is provided for systems where the magnitude of a useful
exponent might exceed the range of an
.BR int .
.PP
The
.B f
suffixed variants
.RB ( scalbnf (),
.BR scalblnf ())
take and return
.B float
values; the
.B l
suffixed variants
.RB ( scalbnl (),
.BR scalblnl ())
take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.PP
These functions perform efficient scaling by manipulating the exponent
field of the floating-point representation directly and do not, in
general, suffer the rounding error inherent in computing
.BI pow( FLT_RADIX ", " n )
followed by a multiplication.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR scalbn ()
and
.BR scalbln ()
return
.IR x " * FLT_RADIX^" n .
.PP
If the result overflows the range of the return type, a range
(overflow) error occurs and the function returns
.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
(or
.B \(+-HUGE_VALF
or
.BR \(+-HUGE_VALL )
with the same sign as
.IR x .
.PP
If the result underflows the range of the return type, a range
(underflow) error occurs and the function returns 0 with the same sign
as
.IR x .
.PP
If
.I x
is \(+-0, \(+-infinity, or
.IR n " is 0,"
.I x
is returned unchanged.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN, a NaN is returned.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Range error: result overflow
An overflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.TP
Range error: result underflow
An underflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_UNDERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR scalbn (),
.BR scalbnf (),
.BR scalbnl (),
.BR scalbln (),
.BR scalblnf (),
.BR scalblnl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR scalbn (),
.BR scalbln (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments follows C99 Annex F.10.3.2 (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program demonstrates basic scaling and the underflow
behaviour at the extreme end of the exponent range:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <errno.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double y;

    /* Ordinary scaling: 1.5 * 2^3 == 12.0 */
    y = scalbn(1.5, 3);
    printf("scalbn(1.5, 3)        = %.6f\en", y);

    /* Underflow to +0 with errno set to ERANGE. */
    errno = 0;
    y = scalbn(1.0, INT_MIN);
    printf("scalbn(1.0, INT_MIN)  = %.6f (errno=%d)\en", y, errno);

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
scalbn(1.5, 3)        = 12.000000
scalbn(1.0, INT_MIN)  = 0.000000 (errno=34)
.EE
.RE
